{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>And behold, the plague has stayed in its appearance.</b> In its appearance, from the language of (Numbers 11:7) ״ועינו כעין הבדולח״, (Ezekiel 1:22) ״כעין הקרח הנורא״. But in Torat Kohanim we learned: I have only 'in its own eyes' — from where do we derive 'in the eyes of his disciple'? Scripture says: ״ואם בעיניו עמד הנתק״. If so, its meaning is: and if in the eyes of the aforementioned priest the plague has stayed, that is, it remained as it was and did not change its place and did not spread, according to the appearance of the priest's eye. And this is a familiar expression in the words of our Rabbis of blessed memory: ״כך אני בעיניך״, and likewise in Scripture (II Kings 1:13) ״תיקר נא נפשי בעיניך״. And Scripture alludes that according to the appearance of his eye the priest judges the spreading, and he need not measure the plague."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:25:09.668700766Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}